[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (4...Nxd4) / Study 1: Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/a9be06ed-db6f-4ef1-be5d-db3a8a766b80/studies/08b89b2a-9293-43ae-b0c8-742466117486/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board.} 5.Qxd4 5...Qf6 {This is the engine's top choice and the second most popular move, played in almost 20% of the games. We don't want to trade the queens, as it would help Black with their development.} 6.Qe3 6...b6 {With ...b6 Black aims to quickly develop the bishop pair. We'll develop our knight, aiming at the d5-square.} 7.Nc3 7...Bc5 {Black attacks our queen, the bishop being supported by the b6-pawn. We'll move the queen to the side. On g3 it is placed well, since it doesn't interfere with our minor piece development.} 8.Qg3 8...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. We'll develop our bishop with an attack on the c7-pawn.} 9.Bf4 9...d6 {Black blocks the attack.} 10.O-O-O {Now we get our king into safety on the queenside. We'll aim to continue developing with Bb5+ and also push e5 to open up the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (4...Nxd4) / Study 1: Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/a9be06ed-db6f-4ef1-be5d-db3a8a766b80/studies/08b89b2a-9293-43ae-b0c8-742466117486/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board.} 5.Qxd4 5...Qf6 {This is the engine's top choice and the second most popular move, played in almost 20% of the games. We don't want to trade the queens, as it would help Black with their development.} 6.Qe3 6...Bb4+ {Black develops with check. We'll block with the pawn, gaining a tempo on the bishop.} 7.c3 7...Ba5 {Black retreats to a5. We'll develop the bishop to d3 from where it supports the e4-pawn.} 8.Bd3 8...Bb6 {Black attacks our queen. We'll retreat to e2, the safest square for our queen. Here the queen also defends f2.} 9.Qe2 9...d6 {Black fights for the e5-square. Since our queenside is yet to be developed, we'll get our king into safety by castling kingside.} 10.O-O 10...Ne7 {Black wants to castle as well. We want to develop the queenside minor pieces quickly! Having played c3 on move seven, we no longer have c3 for our knight.} 11.Nd2 11...O-O {Black castles.} 12.Nc4 {Nc4 aims to win the bishop pair. Once we capture the bishop on b6, we can push f4 to gain more space on the kingside and use the bishop pair to our advantage!} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (4...Nxd4) / Study 1: Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/a9be06ed-db6f-4ef1-be5d-db3a8a766b80/studies/08b89b2a-9293-43ae-b0c8-742466117486/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nf6 {Black goes for the third most popular move 5...Nf6, which is a mistake! It allows us to immediately attack the knight with e5.} 6.e5 6...Qe7 {Black defends the knight by pinning the e-pawn. We'll interfere the pin with the bishop, after which the knight on f6 is under renewed attack.} 7.Be3 7...Ng8 {Black retreats. We'll develop our queen's knight. d5 looks like a great square for it!} 8.Nc3 8...d6 {Black challenges our strong e5-pawn. But we'll centralize the knight and attack the queen.} 9.Nd5 9...Qd7 {Black moves their queen to d7. Black's king is uncastled, so opening up the center is a great idea, and we'll take on d6.} 10.exd6 10...Qxd6 {Black recaptured with the queen to avoid Bb5, which would have won the queen owing to the fork on c7...} 11.Bf4 {...but this allows Bf4! We are attacking the queen, and the pawn on c7 behind it, which will fall next. White is completely winning.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (4...Nxd4) / Study 1: Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/a9be06ed-db6f-4ef1-be5d-db3a8a766b80/studies/08b89b2a-9293-43ae-b0c8-742466117486/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nf6 {Black goes for the third most popular move 5...Nf6, which is a mistake! It allows us to immediately attack the knight with e5.} 6.e5 6...c5 {Black attacks our queen instead of retreating the knight, which occurs in around 20% of the time. We bring the queen to e3 so that ...Qe7 doesn't pin the e-pawn and simply loses the knight.} 7.Qe3 7...Nd5 {Black attacks our queen. We'll bring it to e4, attacking the knight in return.} 8.Qe4 8...Nb6 {Black retreats to b6. We'll develop. Our knight aims at the weak b5 and d5 squares.} 9.Nc3 9...Be7 {Black prepares to castle. Now Bf4 reinforces the pawn on e5, aims at the weakened d6-square and prepares to castle queenside!} 10.Bf4 10...O-O {Black hides their king. But we can develop a piece with a checkmate threat.} 11.Bd3 11...g6 {Black prevents the mate by blocking the battery.} 12.O-O-O {After castling queenside our position is very comfortable. We have more space, better developed pieces and better control over the center. We can start to improve our pieces with Rhe1, Nd5, and Bh6.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (4...Nxd4) / Study 1: Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/a9be06ed-db6f-4ef1-be5d-db3a8a766b80/studies/08b89b2a-9293-43ae-b0c8-742466117486/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board.} 5.Qxd4 5...d6 {And Black goes for the most popular move, 5...d6 played in almost 40% of the games. Black opens up the light-squared bishop and most importantly, fights for the e5-square. We'll develop the knight, fighting for central squares.} 6.Nc3 6...Nf6 {Black develops their knight. By developing the bishop to f4 we add control over the e5-square and prepare to castle queenside.} 7.Bf4 7...Be7 {Black prepares to castle short. We'll get the king into safety and centralize the rook by castling queenside.} 8.O-O-O 8...O-O {Black also gets their king out of the center and into safety.} 9.f3 {And with the move f3, we support the pawn on e4 and prepare a kingside attack with g4 and h4! Kb1 is also a useful move in many positions where we've castled queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 1: The Main Line (4...Nxd4) / Study 1: Main Line Under 2000 (4...Nxd4) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/a9be06ed-db6f-4ef1-be5d-db3a8a766b80/studies/08b89b2a-9293-43ae-b0c8-742466117486/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black goes for ...e5. We'll bring the knight to f3 as is usually done in e4-e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops and protects e5. Now 3.d4 is the signature move of the Scotch! We go for central activity. 3.d4 immediately creates two threats: capturing the pawn on e5 or pushing d5 with a tempo, gaining central space.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades on d4. This happens in nearly 90% of the games, as other moves are inferior. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nxd4 {Black trades again, not allowing us to double their c-pawns. This trade is the most popular move under 2000. Played nearly 45% of the time, the problem with it is that our queen arrives rapidly in the center of the board.} 5.Qxd4 5...Nf6 {Black goes for the third most popular move 5...Nf6, which is a mistake! It allows us to immediately attack the knight with e5.} 6.e5 6...c5 {Black attacks our queen instead of retreating the knight, which occurs in around 20% of the time. We bring the queen to e3 so that ...Qe7 doesn't pin the e-pawn and simply loses the knight.} 7.Qe3 7...Nd5 {Black attacks our queen. We'll bring it to e4, attacking the knight in return.} 8.Qe4 8...Nb4 {Black brings the knight to b4, intending to retreat to c6 in the future. We just develop the knight, aiming at weak light squares in the black camp.} 9.Nc3 9...d5 {Black expands and attacks our queen. But we can capture en passant, with discovered check!} 10.exd6+ 10...Be6 {Black blocks the check.} 11.Bb5+ {Check! And Black's position collapses shortly. They'll have to retreat the knight to c6, where it is attacked twice.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...d6 {In 20% of the games, Black continues with ...d6. They are opening up the c8-bishop for development, and protecting the other bishop on c5. We'll get the king into safety.} 8.O-O 8...Bd7 {Black intends to castle queenside. We'll play Qd2, connecting the rooks and continuing to support the knight on d4.} 9.Qd2 9...O-O-O {Black castles queenside.} 10.b4 {Our plan is pretty straightforward: a queenside pawn storm! We will play a4 and b5. It also comes with tempi on Black's minor pieces.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...O-O {Nearly 40% of the time Black castles here. It's the most natural move in this position. We'll get the king into safety as well. Ideally, we want to trade off some of Black's most active pieces.} 8.O-O 8...d6 {In a quarter of the games, Black opens up the light-squared bishop with ...d6. Moves like ...Bb6 or ...a6 don't really accomplish anything. The pressure against our knight on d4 hinders our development slightly, so we'll remove it by trading on c6.} 9.Nxc6 9...Nxc6 {Blacks keeps their structure intact and recaptures with the knight. Now we'll trade off the dark-squared bishop. It was Black's most active piece, and this trade also doubles Black's c-pawns.} 10.Bxc5 10...dxc5 {Black recaptures. We'll gain some space in the center by pushing f4.} 11.f4 11...Be6 {Black offers to trade the light-squared bishops. We push forward, creating a threat against Black's queen.} 12.e5 12...Qe7 {Black retreats the queen. We'll trade off the bishops, as retreating would be too passive.} 13.Bxe6 13...Qxe6 {Black recaptures with the queen. Our knight comes to d2, where it will either jump to e4 or f3. Both squares are suitable in this position.} 14.Nd2 14...Rad8 {Black pins our knight to the queen. So we'll unpin with Qc2.} 15.Qc2 15...f6 {By playing ...f6, Black challenges our central pawns.} 16.exf6 {We'll trade since the pawn would be lost otherwise. The f-pawn provides some spatial advantage on the kingside and allows for a potential future attack. We have ideas of Nf3 or Ne4. The a-rook will side to e1 to gain control over the e-file.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 10"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Nxd4 {In 10% of the games, Black traded the knights on d4. We'll recapture with the bishop.} 6.Bxd4 6...Bxd4 {Our bishop aimed at the weak g7-pawn, so Black trades also trades the bishops. We recapture. Our queen is nicely centralized now, and Black cannot attack it with any of their minor pieces.} 7.Qxd4 7...Qf6 {...so they offer a queen trade. We'll decline, and attack the queen in return. Trading on f6 would also help Black develop the knight.} 8.e5 8...Qb6 {Black offers a queen trade yet again. Now we'll recapture. Black must damage their pawn structure and our pawn provides some spatial advantage for the endgame.} 9.Qxb6 9...axb6 {Black recaptures, opening the a-file for their rook. Our knight comes out. The d5-square looks great! We also clear the way for castling.} 10.Nc3 10...Ne7 {Black prevents us from jumping into d5. By castling queenside we get our king into safety and also bring the rook into the game.} 11.O-O-O 11...O-O {Black's king gets out of the center.} 12.Bc4 {c4 is a great square for the bishop: it aims at Black's king while also covering the a2-pawn. We'll continue by advancing on the kingside with g3-f4 and centralizing the h1-rook.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...O-O {Nearly 40% of the time Black castles here. It's the most natural move in this position. We'll get the king into safety as well. Ideally, we want to trade off some of Black's most active pieces.} 8.O-O 8...b6 {Very few games continue with ...b6. Now Qd2 prepares to connect the rooks and supports the bishop on e3.} 9.Qd2 9...Bb7 {Black fianchettoes. This places indirect pressure on the e4-pawn.} 10.f4 {And here comes the classic f4-move, gaining some space on the kingside. Ideas of e5 and b4 are still present!} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...O-O {Nearly 40% of the time Black castles here. It's the most natural move in this position. We'll get the king into safety as well. Ideally, we want to trade off some of Black's most active pieces.} 8.O-O 8...b6 {Very few games continue with ...b6. Now Qd2 prepares to connect the rooks and supports the bishop on e3.} 9.Qd2 9...Qg6 {Black decides to attack the pawn on e4 directly. We'll trade on c6, intending to trade off Black's powerful dark-squared bishop next.} 10.Nxc6 10...Qxc6 {Black recaptures with the queen. We'll take on c5 as planned.} 11.Bxc5 11...Qxc5 {If you compare the bishops, our bishop on c4 is much more active.} 12.Na3 {We finish our development by protecting the bishop on c4 and connecting the rooks. We will also centralize our rooks to gain greater control of the center.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...Ne5 {In 20% of the games, Black attacks the bishop on c4 by playing ...Ne5. We'll retreat our bishop to e2, taking away squares from the knight on e5. The idea is to play f4 without allowing ...Ng4.} 8.Be2 8...Qg6 {Black attacks both the e4 and g2-pawns. This occured in 30% of the games. We'll castle and protect g2.} 9.O-O 9...Qxe4 {We lose a pawn but in return, our pieces will get rapid development. We'll start with Nd2, when the queen is attacked.} 10.Nd2 10...Qg6 {Black must move the queen yet again. Let's gain yet another tempo on the queen!} 11.Bh5 11...Qd3 {Black tries to be annoying by keeping their queen in our camp. This happens in almost 60% of the games!} 12.N4f3 {We offer to trade the knights. After ....Nxf3+ Qxf3 and our position is great, with better development than Black. ...Bxe3 Nxe5 ...Qxd2 Bxf7+ and White is once again much better.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...Ne5 {In 20% of the games, Black attacks the bishop on c4 by playing ...Ne5. We'll retreat our bishop to e2, taking away squares from the knight on e5. The idea is to play f4 without allowing ...Ng4.} 8.Be2 8...Qg6 {Black attacks both the e4 and g2-pawns. This occured in 30% of the games. We'll castle and protect g2.} 9.O-O 9...Qxe4 {We lose a pawn but in return, our pieces will get rapid development. We'll start with Nd2, when the queen is attacked.} 10.Nd2 10...Qg6 {Black must move the queen yet again. Let's gain yet another tempo on the queen!} 11.Bh5 11...Qb6 {Black creates a queen-bishop battery, which occurs almost 20% of the time. We'll immediately attack the bishop with b4.} 12.b4 12...Bxd4 {Black trades. Now we'll recapture with the bishop, hitting the knight on e5 and attacking the queen.} 13.Bxd4 13...Qd6 {Black moves the queen.} 14.Re1 {The rook will be very potent on e1, as Black's king is still in the center and there is now an immediate attack on the e5-knight.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 7"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...Ne5 {In 20% of the games, Black attacks the bishop on c4 by playing ...Ne5. We'll retreat our bishop to e2, taking away squares from the knight on e5. The idea is to play f4 without allowing ...Ng4.} 8.Be2 8...d5 {24% of games continue with Black striking in the center with ...d4, posing a question to our pawn on e4. We'll get the king into safety, sacrificing the pawn on e4 but in return, we'll get ahead in development.} 9.O-O 9...dxe4 {Black grabs the "free" pawn, which was played in 70% of the games! Now Nb5 actually threatens a checkmate with Nxc7+ and Qd8#!} 10.Nb5 10...Bd6 {Black covers it. We'll set up a small trap with Bc5. Black cannot trade due to checkmate.} 11.Bc5 11...O-O {Black must get their king out of the center now. We'll regain the material by trading on d6.} 12.Nxd6 12...cxd6 {Black recaptures.} 13.Qxd6 {We've got the pawn back! We'll have control over the e-file, plus we are attacking e7 and threatening to damage Black's pawn structure with Qxf6.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 8"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...Ne5 {In 20% of the games, Black attacks the bishop on c4 by playing ...Ne5. We'll retreat our bishop to e2, taking away squares from the knight on e5. The idea is to play f4 without allowing ...Ng4.} 8.Be2 8...d6 {Black could go for 8...d6, which occurs 20% of the time. We'll simply castle and get our king out of the center.} 9.O-O 9...O-O {Black castles as well.} 10.f4 {And now we gain space on the kingside with f4, with the plans of expanding with b4 and finishing the development.} *

[Event "Chapter 2: The Classical Variation (4...Bc5) / Study 1: Classical Scotch (4...Bc5) / Variation 9"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/d09774ef-fa41-46a8-8bd1-acc0e0176642/studies/a76edffb-358f-4027-ba7b-342ec58fdfce/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. We'll bring out the knight to its natural square in e4, e5 openings.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops in the same manner. And now, after striking in the center with 3.d4, the Scotch is on the board.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {70% of the time, Black trades on d4. We'll recapture.} 4.Nxd4 4...Bc5 {This is the second most popular continuation, occurring in 24% of the games. Black adds an attacker to our knight on d4. We'll support our strong piece on d4 by bringing the bishop out to e3.} 5.Be3 5...Qf6 {By moving the queen to f6, Black adds an attacker to the knight on d4. Alternatively, 5...Bb6 6.Nf5 is promising for us. We can add support to our central knight by playing c3.} 6.c3 6...Nge7 {Black develops the knight to e7 and prepares to castle. We'll also prepare to castle by developing the bishop to c4.} 7.Bc4 7...Qg6 {This is the least popular move, played in less than 10% of the games. Black targets e4 and g2. We'll trade on c6.} 8.Nxc6 8...Qxg2 {Black creates a threat against our rook... ...so we'll bring it to f1 and now Black's bishop on c5 is hanging!} 9.Rf1 9...Bxe3 {Black tries to avoid losing material.} 10.Nxe7 {...but we grab the knight on e7! White has won a piece here.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Study 1: Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/7feaaa82-89b6-4654-b442-df81b96eaba2/studies/86bee547-7b1f-4c47-8389-28243bbea360/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights.} 5.Nxc6 5...bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle.} 6.Bd3 6...d5 {This is the most popular, and also the strongest move Black could go for, played in more than half of the games. Black immediately challenges our central pawn on e4. We'll trade here, getting rid of the pressure and opening our light-squared bishop.} 7.exd5 7...cxd5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. We'll get the king into safety and potentially the rook into action on the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bd6 {Black develops the bishop to d6, which is the second most popular move after ...Be7. We can improve our rook with a check.} 9.Re1+ 9...Be6 {Black blocks the check with the light-squared bishop. Now that Black's light-squared bishop has abandoned its home square (and is pinned), we'll give a check on b5.} 10.Bb5+ 10...Nd7 {Black's only reasonable move is to cover the check with the knight. Now we'll attack both the d5-pawn and the rook on a8.} 11.Bc6 11...Rb8 {Black must move the rook. And now we can win a pawn and increase the pressure on the e6-bishop.} 12.Bxd5 12...O-O {Black gets the king out of the center.} 13.Nc3 {And we develop the knight to c3 and support our strong bishop on d5. We are up a pawn and Black's queenside pawns are looking weak!} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Study 1: Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/7feaaa82-89b6-4654-b442-df81b96eaba2/studies/86bee547-7b1f-4c47-8389-28243bbea360/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights.} 5.Nxc6 5...bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle.} 6.Bd3 6...d5 {This is the most popular, and also the strongest move Black could go for, played in more than half of the games. Black immediately challenges our central pawn on e4. We'll trade here, getting rid of the pressure and opening our light-squared bishop.} 7.exd5 7...cxd5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. We'll get the king into safety and potentially the rook into action on the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Bd6 {Black develops the bishop to d6, which is the second most popular move after ...Be7. We can improve our rook with a check.} 9.Re1+ 9...Be7 {Black retreats the bishop to e7, essentially losing a tempo. We'll create a queen-rook battery aimed at the dark-squared bishop, so that Black cannot castle immediately.} 10.Qe2 10...Be6 {Black blocks the e-file. Now we'll give a check on b5, making use of the light squares yet again.} 11.Bb5+ 11...Nd7 {Black blocks the check. We'll attack the rook and put pressure on the d5-pawn.} 12.Bc6 12...Rb8 {After Black moves the rook...} 13.Nc3 {...we develop the knight and soon the d5-pawn will fall. For example, 13...0-0 and we will capture with 14.Bxd5. We ideally want to take with the bishop so that our pressure on the e-file is felt a lot more.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Study 1: Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/7feaaa82-89b6-4654-b442-df81b96eaba2/studies/86bee547-7b1f-4c47-8389-28243bbea360/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights.} 5.Nxc6 5...bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle.} 6.Bd3 6...Bc5 {Black brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the f2-pawn. This move is not the strongest one, yet it is played 30% of the time. We will push e5 here, kicking the knight away from its natural square and opening up our light-squared bishop.} 7.e5 7...Qe7 {Black pins our pawn to the knight with the queen. We can see this in around 40% of the games. So, naturally, we'll castle, getting the king away from the pin. Black of course cannot grab the pawn on e5, because of the Re1 pin.} 8.O-O 8...Nd5 {Black moves the knight. We'll develop our knight. It will sit nicely on the e4-square.} 9.Nd2 9...O-O {Black gets the king into safety. We can try to win the bishop pair while centralizing our knight...} 10.Ne4 10...Bb6 {...so Black retreats. With c4, we'll gain more space on the queenside and get rid of Black's strong knight on d5.} 11.c4 11...Nb4 {Black puts pressure on our bishop. Their pieces aren't developed well, meanwhile, all of our pieces aim at Black's kingside! This calls for a sacrifice. Now Nf6+ is a very powerful move.} 12.Nf6+ 12...gxf6 {Black grabs the material. And here comes another sacrifice!} 13.Bxh7+ 13...Kxh7 {Black captures. Now we bring the queen into the attack with check. Black's pawns prevent the king running away and limit the defensive scope of their queen!} 14.Qh5+ 14...Kg8 {Black moves to g8. Check again! All of our moves should come with a check or a checkmate threat, so that Black does not have time to mobilize their defense.} 15.Qg4+ 15...Kh7 {The king moves. Another check, this time on h4! Now the dark-squared bishop will come in handy.} 16.Qh4+ 16...Kg8 {The king moves to g8. Now we threaten a checkmate in two. Black can't prevent the coming check on the g-file.} 17.Bh6 17...Qxe5 {This is Black's only resource not to get checkmated immediately. We still have the check on g4.} 18.Qg4+ 18...Qg5 {Black must cover with the queen, otherwise Qg7 is checkmate.} 19.Bxg5 {But now we win the queen.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Study 1: Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/7feaaa82-89b6-4654-b442-df81b96eaba2/studies/86bee547-7b1f-4c47-8389-28243bbea360/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights.} 5.Nxc6 5...bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle.} 6.Bd3 6...Bc5 {Black brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the f2-pawn. This move is not the strongest one, yet it is played 30% of the time. We will push e5 here, kicking the knight away from its natural square and opening up our light-squared bishop.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps to d5, the only logical square for the knight. g4 is a great square for our queen now. It attacks the undefended g7-pawn and will pose some danger to Black if they castle kingside.} 8.Qg4 8...O-O {Black castles to defend the g7-pawn. Other moves are passive. Now Bh6 threatens a checkmate! Black of course cannot capture the bishop: it is pinned due to the good placement of our queen.} 9.Bh6 9...g6 {This is the only move to defend and avoid major material losses. We can grab the rook now and be an exchange up.} 10.Bxf8 10...Qxf8 {Black recaptures. Now it's time to get our king into safety.} 11.O-O 11...d6 {Black opens up an attack on our queen.} 12.Qe4 {The queen is nicely centralized on e4. We'll finish our development and enjoy the material advantage! Nd2 and Re1 are good, improving moves. If 12...Bf5, 13.Qa4 attacks c6 and we have a great position.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Study 1: Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/7feaaa82-89b6-4654-b442-df81b96eaba2/studies/86bee547-7b1f-4c47-8389-28243bbea360/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights.} 5.Nxc6 5...bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle.} 6.Bd3 6...d5 {This is the most popular, and also the strongest move Black could go for, played in more than half of the games. Black immediately challenges our central pawn on e4. We'll trade here, getting rid of the pressure and opening our light-squared bishop.} 7.exd5 7...cxd5 {Black recaptures with the pawn. We'll get the king into safety and potentially the rook into action on the e-file.} 8.O-O 8...Be7 {Black prepares to castle with ...Be7, occurring in almost 70% of the games. Our fianchetto on b2 will prove valuable against Black's kingside.} 9.b3 9...O-O {Black castles. Now we'll play Bb2, with control along the long diagonal.} 10.Bb2 10...c5 {The pawn duo in the center for Black appears to be advantageous, but the position is equal. With h3, we ensure that Black won't get access to g4.} 11.h3 {We will now continue our development with moves like Re1 and Nd2. Bf1 can be played if we need it to put our bishop on a safe square and defend our kingside. This is an equal position, but our moves are easy to find. Black can make a strategic mistake with ...d4, giving us the c4-square as an outpost for a piece.} *

[Event "Chapter 3: The Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Study 1: Schmidt Variation (4...Nf6) / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/7feaaa82-89b6-4654-b442-df81b96eaba2/studies/86bee547-7b1f-4c47-8389-28243bbea360/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black copies us. We'll develop, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black defends their e-pawn. With d4, our opening is now on the board! The Scotch.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black trades in the center. We'll recapture with the knight.} 4.Nxd4 4...Nf6 {And Black develops the knight to f6, eyeing our e4-pawn. At the club level, this is the third most popular move, but in the Masters database, it's the most played one. This is the engine's top choice. We'll trade off the knights.} 5.Nxc6 5...bxc6 {Black's pawns are doubled... ...and now we'll develop with Bd3, supporting the pawn on e4 and preparing to castle.} 6.Bd3 6...Bc5 {Black brings the bishop to c5, eyeing the f2-pawn. This move is not the strongest one, yet it is played 30% of the time. We will push e5 here, kicking the knight away from its natural square and opening up our light-squared bishop.} 7.e5 7...Nd5 {Black jumps to d5, the only logical square for the knight. g4 is a great square for our queen now. It attacks the undefended g7-pawn and will pose some danger to Black if they castle kingside.} 8.Qg4 8...g6 {This is the top move in the database, played 40% of the time. It defends g7, but creates some dark-squared weaknesses. We'll exploit them by placing our bishop on h6, preventing Black from castling.} 9.Bh6 9...d6 {...d6 opens up an attack on our queen. We'll shift the queen to the queenside, attacking the c6-pawn.} 10.Qa4 10...Bd7 {Black defends it. We'll get the king into safety and prepare to activate the rook.} 11.O-O 11...dxe5 {Black closes the e-file by capturing on e5.} 12.Bg7 {But now we get a strong hold of the center and quick development with Black's castling nonexistent! We will win a pawn and then it's up to us to consolidate by developing our pieces with moves like Nc3 and Re1, and holding on to the pawn advantage.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 4 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/3e5c46ea-8791-49c8-9e7c-a57c7c3debaa/studies/4421f114-2421-4c91-b4d3-c4d085cd612b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The knight gets out and attacks the central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {And Black defends it. With d4 we reach the Scotch Game.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. And we will recapture. All this we've seen before.} 4.Nxd4 4...Qf6 {The move ...Qf6 is probably positionally the best sideline. It's played 5% of the time, which is still almost 2.5 million games! Black is attacking our knight once again and often wants to slide to g6 to keep the queen active. We'll protect the knight for now, with c3.} 5.c3 5...Qg6 {Capturing on d4 would be a mistake, giving us strong central pawns. Instead, a logical move is to move to g6, attacking e4, and freeing f6 for the knight. What they might not know is that we can let them capture the pawn! Because the queen has come out early, Nb5 is an extremely potent move, targeting the pawn on c7 and threatening a fork.} 6.Nb5 6...Qxe4+ {Even if Black captures on e4... It just gives us more room to develop, while blocking the check. You might notice that this move sacrifices the g2-pawn as well. Well, this double sacrifice is actually the way to go for White.} 7.Be2 7...Qxg2 {Because White is winning if Black takes this pawn. We can defend our rook with Bf3, also attacking the black queen, and the fork on c7 is still a problem for Black.} 8.Bf3 8...Qh3 {The queen has to escape to a square like h3... After which we can fork Black with the capture on c7.} 9.Nxc7+ 9...Kd8 {And once the king moves... We'll grab the rook. A good trade for two pawns.} 10.Nxa8 10...Ne5 {Sure, Black could continue the aggression with ...Ne5, attacking our bishop. But they are playing with the only two pieces they have activated in the opening.} 11.Be2 {We will just retreat and enjoy a winning position. Black has no more threats and we want to centralize the queen on d5 and play Bf4 to attack the knight and take the diagonal. It's not even going to be easy for Black to win our knight on a8.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 4 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/3e5c46ea-8791-49c8-9e7c-a57c7c3debaa/studies/4421f114-2421-4c91-b4d3-c4d085cd612b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The knight gets out and attacks the central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {And Black defends it. With d4 we reach the Scotch Game.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. And we will recapture. All this we've seen before.} 4.Nxd4 4...Qh4 {The move ...Qh4 is played only 2% of the time, but does have some merit to it. In fact, Black has a bigger win rate than White in this line in the database. The queen attacks the e4-pawn. We can defend it with the knight, but Black has an answer to that too.} 5.Nc3 5...Bb4 {And that's to pin our defender. Now the threat on the knight is reintroduced. We don't want to complicate things by playing Nxc6, allowing Qe4+. In fact, the best move is to actually develop and pre-emptively block the incoming capture so that it no longer arrives with check.} 6.Be2 6...Qxe4 {They can take the pawn here, but this is actually better for White. The queen leaving its post early has left the c7-pawn unattended. So we'll jump to b5 and attack the c7-pawn. It's actually not that easy to defend.} 7.Nb5 7...Bxc3+ {Black takes on c3. We don't want to recapture with the knight and allow them to capture on g2. That would be bad for us. Instead, we'll take with the pawn and maintain the threat to c7.} 8.bxc3 8...Kd8 {Now Black basically has to side-step to d8 and protect the pawn. Other than that, there aren't many good moves.} 9.O-O {In that case, we will just castle, and let's take a look at what we got in this position. We are down a pawn, but we've acquired the bishop pair. Apart from that, we have more space to develop and a lead in activity, while Black has lost their castling rights. White is better here.} *

[Event "Chapter 4: Move 4 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 4 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/3e5c46ea-8791-49c8-9e7c-a57c7c3debaa/studies/4421f114-2421-4c91-b4d3-c4d085cd612b/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds with ...e5. The knight gets out and attacks the central pawn.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {And Black defends it. With d4 we reach the Scotch Game.} 3.d4 3...exd4 {Black takes on d4. And we will recapture. All this we've seen before.} 4.Nxd4 4...d5 {The move ...d5 is only played 1% of the time, and it doesn't accomplish much for Black. We will take on d5 and release the unnecessary tension in the center.} 5.exd5 5...Qxd5 {Sure, Black can activate their queen and attack the knight...} 6.Qe2+ {But we don't have to play by their rules and permit a queen trade. The best move for Black is to block with the bishop on e6 and allow us to capture it. Otherwise, if they block with the bishop or the knight on e7, White is already winning after Nb5, when the queen would have to retreat passively to defend c7.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 5"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/96899f8b-e46f-42a2-8507-00e61026d61d/studies/6376bab7-b03f-456d-b744-0e2997dfdf19/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black goes for a central counter-attack with 3...d5. This move occurs very rarely. We can grab the pawn on e5, getting our knight to a strong square at the same time.} 4.Nxe5 4...Nxe5 {Black trades, which happens in 60% of the time. We'll recapture the knight.} 5.dxe5 5...dxe4 {Black must regain the pawn, or they'll just be a pawn down without any real compensation. Trading the queens is good for us here, since Black will lose their castling rights in an otherwise symmetrical position.} 6.Qxd8+ 6...Kxd8 {Black recaptures the queen.} 7.Nc3 {And after developing the knight we'll target the e4-pawn, bring the bishops to c4 and g5 and enjoy our castling rights!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 4"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/96899f8b-e46f-42a2-8507-00e61026d61d/studies/6376bab7-b03f-456d-b744-0e2997dfdf19/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3.d4 3...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, counter-attacking our pawn on e4. This is the third most popular move, occurring 7% of the time, representing over 1 million games! We'll win a pawn on e5 and attack the knight.} 4.dxe5 4...Nxe4 {Black regains the pawn. Now c4 is a great square for our light-squared bishop. We immediately start to eye the weak f7-pawn. There are ideas of bringing the queen to d5 and threatening checkmate!} 5.Bc4 5...d6 {Black offers another pawn for a quick development. ...d6 is the fourth most popular move. Now we can sacrifice our bishop on f7. The motif of bringing our queen to d5 is apparent yet again!} 6.Bxf7+ 6...Kxf7 {Black recaptures. Our queen now arrives on d5 with check, forking the king and the e4-knight.} 7.Qd5+ 7...Be6 {Black attacks our queen and blocks the check. We'll regain the piece.} 8.Qxe4 8...dxe5 {Black recaptures on e5 with the pawn to free up their pieces and go for some activity. 8...Be7 was also possible. But their king isn't safe! We must keep attacking here, or Black will escape. Thus we'll play Ng5+.} 9.Ng5+ 9...Ke7 {Blacks moves the king and must also guard the e6-bishop. We'll grab the bishop, luring Black's king into the open.} 10.Nxe6 10...Kxe6 {Black recaptures.} 11.Nc3 {We can just develop here. Black's king is feeling very exposed and our knight will most likely end up on the d5-square. We can choose to castle either side, depending on Black's plan of hiding their king!} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 3"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/96899f8b-e46f-42a2-8507-00e61026d61d/studies/6376bab7-b03f-456d-b744-0e2997dfdf19/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3.d4 3...Nf6 {Black develops the knight, counter-attacking our pawn on e4. This is the third most popular move, occurring 7% of the time, representing over 1 million games! We'll win a pawn on e5 and attack the knight.} 4.dxe5 4...Nxe4 {Black regains the pawn. Now c4 is a great square for our light-squared bishop. We immediately start to eye the weak f7-pawn. There are ideas of bringing the queen to d5 and threatening checkmate!} 5.Bc4 5...Bc5 {Black piles up on the f2-square. But f7 is also a target. With Qd5, we continue with our plan, and a checkmate is now threatened!} 6.Qd5 6...Bxf2+ {Black captures on f2 with a check...} 7.Ke2 {...but after Ke2, Black's positions falls apart, since they cannot defend both of their pieces and a checkmate threat on f7 at the same time.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 1"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/96899f8b-e46f-42a2-8507-00e61026d61d/studies/6376bab7-b03f-456d-b744-0e2997dfdf19/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3.d4 3...d6 {This move is played only 10% of the time, but it is the second most popular move! Black's idea is to support the e5-pawn and avoid opening up the center. We can now gain space in the center with a tempo on Black's c6-knight.} 4.d5 4...Nce7 {Back retreats to e7 instead of jumping straight back to b8. If instead ...Nb4, we have a3, and after ...Na6 then Bxa6 when we have seriously damaged Black's pawn structure. After Nce7 we'll add to our pawn center with c4.} 5.c4 5...Nf6 {Black brings out the knight with an attack on the e4-pawn. We'll develop. c3 is a natural square for the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop to g4, pinning our knight on f3. We'll ask the bishop a question with h3.} 7.h3 7...Bh5 {Black retreats to h5 to keep the pin. We'll expand on the kingside and gain time on the bishop.} 8.g4 8...Bg6 {They retreat to g6. Now we develop a piece with Bd3 and support the pawn on e4.} 9.Bd3 9...c6 {Black tries to undermine our pawn chain.} 10.Be3 {But we can simply bring yet another piece to a better square. Black's pieces are looking very cramped on the kingside since we have a space advantage there. Our plan can revolve around gaining further space there and hiding our king on the queenside.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 2"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/96899f8b-e46f-42a2-8507-00e61026d61d/studies/6376bab7-b03f-456d-b744-0e2997dfdf19/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3.d4 3...d6 {This move is played only 10% of the time, but it is the second most popular move! Black's idea is to support the e5-pawn and avoid opening up the center. We can now gain space in the center with a tempo on Black's c6-knight.} 4.d5 4...Nce7 {Back retreats to e7 instead of jumping straight back to b8. If instead ...Nb4, we have a3, and after ...Na6 then Bxa6 when we have seriously damaged Black's pawn structure. After Nce7 we'll add to our pawn center with c4.} 5.c4 5...Nf6 {Black brings out the knight with an attack on the e4-pawn. We'll develop. c3 is a natural square for the knight.} 6.Nc3 6...Bg4 {Black develops the bishop to g4, pinning our knight on f3. We'll ask the bishop a question with h3.} 7.h3 7...Bxf3 {Instead of retreating, Black immediately captures the knight on f3 which happens in around 50% of the games. We should recapture with the queen to keep our pawn structure intact.} 8.Qxf3 8...Ng6 {Black improves the knight and opens up e7 for the bishop. Against a knight on g6, the h4 push is usually very powerful! We are threatening to push even further with h5, attacking the knight on g6.} 9.h4 9...h5 {Black stops our intended advance. Now g3 acomplishes three things: supports the pawn on h4, protects the f4-square and allows for our light-squared bishop to be developed.} 10.g3 10...Be7 {Black prepares to castle. Black is without a light-squared bishop, so our own bishop is a very powerful piece! We'll activate it on h3.} 11.Bh3 11...O-O {Black gets their king into safety.} 12.Bg5 {After Bg5 we can plan to capture the knight on f6 and grab the pawn on h5 next. White is doing very well here, as we have a space advantage on the kingside, and our pieces are better placed than Black's.} *

[Event "Chapter 5: Move 3 Sidelines / Study 1: Move 3 Sidelines / Variation 6"]
[Site "https://chessly.com/courses/7723083f-a0d5-4006-bb1a-78402ea0becd/chapters/96899f8b-e46f-42a2-8507-00e61026d61d/studies/6376bab7-b03f-456d-b744-0e2997dfdf19/lines"]

1.e4 1...e5 {Black responds symmetrically. We'll develop the knight, attacking e5.} 2.Nf3 2...Nc6 {Black develops the knight and supports e5. Now 3.d4 is the Scotch!} 3.d4 3...d5 {Black goes for a central counter-attack with 3...d5. This move occurs very rarely. We can grab the pawn on e5, getting our knight to a strong square at the same time.} 4.Nxe5 4...dxe4 {Black continues with the second most popular move which is grabbing the pawn on e4. We'll put pressure on the c6-knight by pinning it.} 5.Bb5 5...Bd7 {Black defends it with the bishop. As Black was threatening to capture the knight on e5, we'll get rid of the bishop and keep the knight on c6 pinned!} 6.Nxd7 6...Qxd7 {Black recaptures.} 7.c3 {And with c3 we support the pawn on d4 while preparing to target the e4-pawn by developing and castling kingside. The c6-knight remains pinned.} *